A middleware is a function that takes a handler and returns a new one. This is Taskboard's simplest:

```go
func secureHeaders(next http.Handler) http.Handler {
	return http.HandlerFunc(func(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
		h := w.Header()
		h.Set("X-Content-Type-Options", "nosniff")
		h.Set("Referrer-Policy", "no-referrer")
		if strings.HasPrefix(r.URL.Path, "/v1/") {
			h.Set("Cache-Control", "no-store")
		}
		next.ServeHTTP(w, r)
	})
}
```

Code before `next.ServeHTTP` runs on the way in, and code after it runs on the way out, once the handler has written its response. Wrapping is just function calls, so the handler you wrap last is the outermost, and it runs first.

To log a status, a middleware has to see it, and a `ResponseWriter` doesn't tell you what was written. So wrap it in a small type that records the status in `WriteHeader`, and give that type an `Unwrap` method, so `http.ResponseController` can still reach the real writer.

